The action of a target binding travels in the options.

Interface Builder offers two ways to wire a menu item to code. The familiar one is a control
connector, which carries the selector directly and which we decode. The other is the target
BINDING, where the destination object arrives as the binding destination and the selector arrives
as the NSSelectorName entry of the options dictionary.

We established the binding and applied its value, so the item ended up with the right target, and
then we dropped the options on the floor. An item with a target and no action is disabled, so the
command was not merely wrong, it was unreachable.

MoneyMoney binds exactly ten menu items this way, and those ten are exactly the ten that measured
with a real target and no action from any setter:

    About MoneyMoney              AboutWindowController    showAboutWindow:
    Encrypt File for Sending...   MainWindowController     encryptFileForSupport:
    Help and FAQ...               MainWindowController     openGeneralHelp:
    Imprint...                    MainWindowController     showImprint:
    License Agreement...          MainWindowController     showLicenseAgreement:
    Privacy Policy...             MainWindowController     showPrivacyPolicy:
    Report an Issue...            MainWindowController     reportIssue:
    Show Database in Finder...    MainWindowController     openDatabaseInFinder:
    Supported Banks (Europe)...   MainWindowController     openPsd2Help:
    Supported Banks (Germany)...  MainWindowController     openBankHelp:

The whole application menu and the whole Help menu, which is why the symptom looked like a menu
defect rather than a bindings one.

doubleClickTarget is the documented sibling and takes the same option, so it is handled here for
the same reason, but no roster application exercises it yet and this change does not claim it
measured.

--- a/AppKit/NSKeyValueBinding/NSObject+BindingSupport.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSKeyValueBinding/NSObject+BindingSupport.m
@@ -301,6 +301,39 @@
     [binder setOptions: options];
 
     [binder bind];
+
+    [self _bindingApplySelectorNameOption: binding options: options];
+}
+
+/* THE SELECTOR TRAVELS IN THE OPTIONS, not in a control connector, which is the only wiring this
+ * port used to understand. Ten MoneyMoney commands reached us with a target and no action, and a
+ * menu item with no action is disabled and dead. */
+- (void) _bindingApplySelectorNameOption: (NSString *) binding
+                                 options: (NSDictionary *) options
+{
+    SEL setter;
+
+    if ([binding isEqualToString: @"target"])
+        setter = @selector(setAction:);
+    else if ([binding isEqualToString: @"doubleClickTarget"])
+        setter = @selector(setDoubleAction:);
+    else
+        return;
+
+    NSString *name = [options objectForKey: @"NSSelectorName"];
+
+    if (![name isKindOfClass: [NSString class]] || [name length] == 0)
+        return;
+    if (![self respondsToSelector: setter])
+        return;
+
+    SEL action = NSSelectorFromString(name);
+
+    if (action == NULL)
+        return;
+
+    void (*apply)(id, SEL, SEL) = (void (*)(id, SEL, SEL)) objc_msgSend;
+    apply(self, setter, action);
 }
 
 - (void) unbind: (id) binding {
